{"name":"Village Green","hex":"#7e867c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"village green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A patch of land at the centre of a village that is open to the public at all times."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/village_green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Village Green, New York","blocks":[{"text":"Village Green is a hamlet in Onondaga County, New York, United States. As of the 2020 census, Village Green had a population of 3,834."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Onondaga_County_New_York_incorporated_and_unincorporated_areas_Village_Green_highlighted.svg/330px-Onondaga_County_New_York_incorporated_and_unincorporated_areas_Village_Green_highlighted.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village_Green%2C_New_York"},{"title":"Village Green, Los Angeles","blocks":[{"text":"Village Green, originally named Baldwin Hills Village, is a neighborhood at the foot of Baldwin Hills, within the city of Los Angeles, California. Village Green consists of a large condominium complex that is both a Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument and a National Historic Landmark. Designed in the late 1930s and completed by 1942, it is one of the oldest planned communities of its type in the nation. Village Green was named by The American Institute of Architects as one of the 100 most important architectural achievements in U.S. history."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Village_Green_Signage.jpg/330px-Village_Green_Signage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village_Green%2C_Los_Angeles"},{"title":"Village Green (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Village Green\" is a song by the English rock band the Kinks from their 1968 album The Kinks Are the Village Green Preservation Society. Written and sung by the band's principal songwriter, Ray Davies, the song was first recorded in November 1966 during the sessions for Something Else by the Kinks (1967) but was re-recorded in February 1967. Both the composition and instrumentation of \"Village Green\" evoke Baroque music, especially its prominently featured harpsichord played by the session keyboardist Nicky Hopkins. Unlike most of the band's late 1960s recordings, it employs real orchestral instruments, including oboe, cello, viola and piccolo, as arranged by the English composer David Whitaker."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/57/Village_Green_Japanese_Kinks_Single.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village_Green_(song)"},{"title":"Village green","blocks":[{"text":"A village green is a common open area within a village or other settlement. Historically, a village green was common grassland with a pond for watering cattle and other animals, often at the edge of a rural settlement, used for gathering cattle to bring them later on to a common land for grazing. Later, planned greens were built into the centres of villages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Chipperfield%2C_War_Memorial_and_The_Two_Brewers_-_geograph.org.uk_-_122311.jpg/330px-Chipperfield%2C_War_Memorial_and_The_Two_Brewers_-_geograph.org.uk_-_122311.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village_green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Village_Green_Photograph.jpg/120px-Village_Green_Photograph.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Village_Green_Photograph.jpg/250px-Village_Green_Photograph.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm